# backend_sweep_2026-09-02 — zenravif (zenrav1e) vs svtav1-rs: RD + cross-decoder gate,
# first run taken against GIT-REV PINNED siblings

Harness: examples/encode_backend_sweep.rs at zenavif `912e5f0` (main).
Pins: zenav1-svt ef0b122b, zenav1-aom 14124356, rav1d-safe 66f58fa6,
zenravif f6c883b6. rustc 1.98.0.

Box: Apple M4 Pro, 12 cores, macOS (Darwin 25.5.0 arm64), `nice -n 19`,
`--threads 4`, `--reps 1`. Built with a plain `cargo build --release` — NO
`-C target-cpu=native`, per the repo integrity rules.

## Why this dataset exists separately from backend_sweep_2026-07-22

Every earlier backend dataset was measured against sibling **working copies**
(`svtav1 = { path = "../zenav1-svt/rust/svtav1" }`). On 2026-09-02 that
checkout carried 71 uncommitted lines in `svtav1-encoder`
(`inter_md_arm.rs`, `pipeline.rs`) — so those numbers describe an encoder that
existed on one disk. Both backend deps are git-rev pins as of `85af725`, and
this is the first sweep where the encoder under test is identified by a commit.
Do not diff this against 2026-07-22 and attribute the delta: the pins, the
box (M4 Pro vs a 16-core cloud VM), the speed set and the image count all
differ.

## Grid

8 CID22-512 validation images x sizes {64, 128, 256, 512} x quality 5..=100
step 5 (20 points, low-q covered at the same density as high-q) x speed 6 x
arms {zenravif-420, zenravif-444, svt-rs-420}. 640 cells x 3 = 1920 rows.

Sizes are all multiples of 64 here on purpose; the partial-superblock envelope
is the companion dataset `backend_sweep_partialsb_2026-09-02.*`.

## Columns

image, size (requested square), width, height (actual), backend, speed,
quality, bytes (encoded AVIF file size), encode_ms, decode_ms, ssim2
(SSIMULACRA2 of the decoded AVIF vs the source), aom_gate.

encode_ms / decode_ms come from a 4-thread parallel run and are therefore
LOAD-POLLUTED in absolute terms. The RATIO between arms is still usable
because the arms are interleaved within the same run under the same load; a
serial `--reps` run is the way to get absolute timings.

## Cross-decoder gate

Every encoded cell's AV1 payload was decoded with BOTH `DecodeBackend::Rav1dSafe`
and `DecodeBackend::AomRs` and the planes byte-compared:

    1920 / 1920 identical

That is the aom decode backend's headline result on this box: zero divergence
on 1920 bitstreams produced by two different encoders across the full quality
range at four sizes.

## Findings

Bytes at MATCHED ssim2 (log-interpolated per image, median across images),
svt-rs-420 / zenravif-420 — below 1.0 means svt-rs is smaller:

     size   ssim2 50   60      70      80      90
       64      1.012  0.954   1.054   0.957   0.936
      128      0.978  1.054   1.049   1.106   1.041
      256      1.087  1.107   1.107   1.126   1.038
      512      1.146  1.156   1.146   1.126   1.028

svt-rs is at rough parity at 64-128 and costs 3-16% more bytes at 256-512,
with the gap closing again at ssim2 90. Median-bytes-vs-median-ssim2 across a
quality sweep is NOT a valid RD comparison and is deliberately not quoted here.

Median encode wall time, and the speed ratio zenravif-420 / svt-rs-420:

     size   svt-rs   zenravif   ratio
       64   0.47 ms    8.11 ms   17.3x
      128   1.64 ms   38.71 ms   23.6x
      256   6.87 ms  184.22 ms   26.8x
      512  24.50 ms  690.59 ms   28.2x

Both at zenavif speed 6 (SVT preset 7). The two backends do not mean the same
thing by "speed 6" — this is a product-level comparison of the two arms as a
caller would select them, not an iso-effort one.

## Repro

    cargo build --release --example encode_backend_sweep \
      --features encode,encode-imazen,encode-svt-rs,aom-backend,target-quality
    nice -n 19 ./target/release/examples/encode_backend_sweep \
      --corpus <codec-corpus>/CID22/CID22-512/validation \
      --images 8 --sizes 64,128,256,512 --qualities 5..=100:5 \
      --speeds 6 --threads 4 --out benchmarks/backend_sweep_2026-09-02.tsv
